course: dictionary
lesson: 5226
title: Jellyfish: a soft sea animal
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A jellyfish is a soft sea animal with a see-through body shaped like an umbrella. Its long, hanging tentacles can sting. «Box jellyfish have tentacles covered in tiny stinging cells.» Context tells you tentacles are the long parts that hang down and sting.
explain: Look for clues in the words around a word. «A jellyfish on the beach can still sting even if it looks dead.» The word still and the word sting tell you that a jellyfish can be dangerous even when it looks harmless.
explain: Some words sound like they belong to one thing but describe another. «Plastic bags look like jellyfish to sea turtles.» Looks like tells you the turtle is fooled, because a bag is not really a jellyfish.
fact: A jellyfish is not a fish: it has no bones, brain, heart or gills.
fact: Jellyfish catch their food with their stinging tentacles.
fact: Sea turtles eat jellyfish, and they may swallow plastic bags that look like them.
fact: Box jellyfish have very strong stings, and in Australia lifeguards may pour vinegar on some stings.
fact: One jellyfish, many jellyfish or jellyfishes: the word can stay the same in the plural.
exercise: What is a jellyfish? | A small fish with bones and gills | *A soft sea animal with a see-through, umbrella-shaped body and stinging tentacles | A kind of seaweed that grows on rocks | A plastic bag floating in the sea
exercise: «Sea turtles eat jellyfish, and floating plastic bags look like jellyfish.» Why might a turtle swallow a plastic bag? | Because bags are a kind of seaweed | Because bags taste of salt | *Because it mistakes the bag for a jellyfish | Because it wants to fill up its shell
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Box jellyfish have tentacles covered in tiny stinging cells» that means long parts that hang down. | vinegar | *tentacles | Australia | lifeguards
test: «A jellyfish on the beach can still sting even if it looks dead.» What does this warn us to do? | *Never touch a jellyfish, even on the sand | Wait until it moves before you go near it | Swim close to a jellyfish because it is safe | Pick up a dead jellyfish to look at it
test: Why is a jellyfish not a fish? | It lives only in rivers | It has bones and gills like a fish | *It has no bones, brain, heart or gills | It has feathers and wings
exam: What should you do if a jellyfish stings you at the beach? | Keep playing until the sting goes away | Swim straight back out to sea | Pull the tentacles off with your bare hands | *Get help from a lifeguard
